      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1886, the Atchison, Topeka & Santa Fe Railroad built a massive freight house in Elgin to process the booming cattle shipments.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://legendsofkansas.com/elgin-kansas/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Elgin became one of the world's busiest cattle shipping towns, earning the nickname 'Cattle Capital of the World'.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elgin%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today, fewer than 90 people live in Elgin, a ghost town where abandoned buildings and an old water tower guard the historic main street.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/elgin-kansas-ghost-town/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Elgin's historic Main Street still hosts an abandoned bank building that once served the bustling cattle trade.",
